1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,367
Coleman:

2
00:00:00,367 --> 00:00:01,650
Hi Coleman, how are you?

3
00:00:01,650 --> 00:00:01,915
Coleman:

4
00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:03,100
Good, thanks.

5
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:03,381
Woman:

6
00:00:03,381 --> 00:00:07,575
I wanted to have a chat with you because our friend Josh told me

7
00:00:07,575 --> 00:00:12,397
that you’ve joined a guitar group and it sounds interesting.

8
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:14,900
I’d really like to learn myself.

9
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:14,950
Coleman:

10
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:16,912
Why don’t you come along?

11
00:00:16,912 --> 00:00:19,500
I’m sure there’s room for another person。

12
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:20,114
Woman:

13
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:20,729
Really?

14
00:00:20,729 --> 00:00:23,800
So – who runs the classes?

15
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:24,334
Coleman:

16
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:29,670
He’s called a ‘coordinator’ – his name’s Gary Mathieson.

17
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:30,165
Woman:

18
00:00:30,165 --> 00:00:32,638
Let me note that down.

19
00:00:32,638 --> 00:00:33,132
Gary.

20
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:33,182
.

21
00:00:33,182 --> 00:00:33,232
.

22
00:00:33,232 --> 00:00:33,282
.

23
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:36,100
How do you spell his surname?

24
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:38,730
Coleman:

25
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:46,620
It’s M-A-T-H-I-E-S-O-N.

26
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:47,303
Woman:

27
00:00:47,303 --> 00:00:48,670
Right, thanks.

28
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:49,037
Coleman:

29
00:00:49,037 --> 00:00:50,506
He’s retired, actually,

30
00:00:50,506 --> 00:00:56,015
but he’s a really nice guy and he used to play in a lot

31
00:00:56,015 --> 00:00:56,750
of bands.

32
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:57,274
Woman:

33
00:00:57,274 --> 00:00:57,799
Thanks.

34
00:00:57,799 --> 00:01:01,470
So how long have you been going?

35
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:01,910
Coleman:

36
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:03,670
About a month now.

37
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:04,126
Woman:

38
00:01:04,126 --> 00:01:07,770
And could you play anything before you started?

39
00:01:07,770 --> 00:01:08,098
Coleman:

40
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:09,738
I knew a few chords,

41
00:01:09,738 --> 00:01:11,050
but that’s all.

42
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:11,340
Woman:

43
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,950
I’m sure everyone will be better than me.

44
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:14,311
Coleman:

45
00:01:14,311 --> 00:01:16,478
That’s what I thought, too.

46
00:01:16,478 --> 00:01:21,535
When I first spoke to Gary on the phone, he said it was a

47
00:01:21,535 --> 00:01:22,619
class for beginners,

48
00:01:22,619 --> 00:01:26,953
but I was still worried that everyone would be better than me,

49
00:01:26,953 --> 00:01:29,120
but we were all equally hopeless.

50
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:29,630
Woman:

51
00:01:29,630 --> 00:01:30,140
Oh,

52
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,670
that’s reassuring.

53
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:34,220
So where do you meet?

54
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:34,593
Coleman:

55
00:01:34,593 --> 00:01:34,966
Well,

56
00:01:34,966 --> 00:01:39,440
when I joined the group, they were meeting in Gary’s home,

57
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:44,660
but as the group got bigger, he decided to book a room at the

58
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:45,779
college in town.

59
00:01:45,779 --> 00:01:47,270
I prefer going there.

60
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:47,663
Woman:

61
00:01:47,663 --> 00:01:49,233
I know that place.

62
00:01:49,233 --> 00:01:54,728
I used to go to tap dancing classes there when I was at secondary

63
00:01:54,728 --> 00:01:55,120
school.

64
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:55,529
Woman:

65
00:01:55,529 --> 00:02:02,066
I haven’t been since, though and I can’t remember what road it’s in

66
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:02,116
.

67
00:02:02,116 --> 00:02:02,166
.

68
00:02:02,166 --> 00:02:02,216
.

69
00:02:02,216 --> 00:02:03,700
is it Lock Street?

70
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:04,119
Coleman:

71
00:02:04,119 --> 00:02:09,981
It’s just beyond there at the bottom of New Street near the city

72
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:10,400
roundabout.

73
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:10,950
Woman:

74
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:12,600
Yes, of course.

75
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:13,156
Coleman:

76
00:02:13,156 --> 00:02:19,270
The guitar club is on the first floor in Room T347.

77
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:19,799
Woman:

78
00:02:19,799 --> 00:02:20,328
Right.

79
00:02:20,328 --> 00:02:22,974
And when do you meet?

80
00:02:22,974 --> 00:02:25,620
Is it at the weekend?

81
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:26,115
Coleman:

82
00:02:26,115 --> 00:02:28,093
We meet on Thursdays.

83
00:02:28,093 --> 00:02:33,533
It used to be 10.30 and that suited me well,

84
00:02:33,533 --> 00:02:36,500
but now we meet at 11.

85
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:36,800
Coleman:

86
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:41,300
The class that’s in there before us asked if they could have the room

87
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:42,500
for another 30 minutes.

88
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:42,944
Woman:

89
00:02:42,944 --> 00:02:44,276
Oh, I see.

90
00:02:44,276 --> 00:02:46,941
Well, I’d love to come,

91
00:02:46,941 --> 00:02:50,050
but I don’t have a guitar.

92
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:50,549
Coleman:

93
00:02:50,549 --> 00:02:54,537
Well, you can always buy a second-hand one.

94
00:02:54,537 --> 00:03:02,016
There’s a website called ‘The perfect instrument’ that sells all kinds of guitars, violins

95
00:03:02,016 --> 00:03:03,511
and so on.

96
00:03:03,511 --> 00:03:07,500
I’m sure you’ll find something there.

97
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:07,805
Woman:

98
00:03:07,805 --> 00:03:10,550
So what’s a typical lesson like with Gary?

99
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:11,010
Coleman:

100
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:16,070
Well, he always starts by getting us to tune our guitars.

101
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:18,370
That takes about five minutes.

102
00:03:18,370 --> 00:03:19,010
Woman:

103
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:19,650
Uhuh.

104
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:20,084
Coleman:

105
00:03:20,084 --> 00:03:23,118
Some people have an app they use,

106
00:03:23,118 --> 00:03:25,719
but others do it by ear.

107
00:03:25,719 --> 00:03:28,320
Gary goes round and helps them.

108
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:28,631
Coleman:

109
00:03:28,631 --> 00:03:33,599
And while he’s doing that, he tells us what he’s going to do during

110
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:34,220
the lesson.

111
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:35,010
Woman:

112
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:35,800
Right.

113
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:36,355
Coleman:

114
00:03:36,355 --> 00:03:41,900
First, we usually spend about ten minutes doing some strumming.

115
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:42,607
Woman:

116
00:03:42,607 --> 00:03:45,435
So is that using .

117
00:03:45,435 --> 00:03:45,485
.

118
00:03:45,485 --> 00:03:45,535
.

119
00:03:45,535 --> 00:03:48,263
what are they called .

120
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:48,313
.

121
00:03:48,313 --> 00:03:48,363
.

122
00:03:48,363 --> 00:03:48,970
plectrums?

123
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:49,506
Coleman:

124
00:03:49,506 --> 00:03:52,720
No – we just use our thumbs.

125
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:53,430
Woman:

126
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:54,850
Much easier.

127
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:55,225
Coleman:

128
00:03:55,225 --> 00:03:59,350
Gary reminds us where to put our fingers for each chord

129
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:01,600
and then we play them together.

130
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:02,069
Coleman:

131
00:04:02,069 --> 00:04:08,636
Sometimes we all just start laughing because we’re so bad at keeping time,

132
00:04:08,636 --> 00:04:11,920
so Gary starts clapping to help us.

133
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:12,267
Woman:

134
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:14,700
Do you learn to play any songs?

135
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:15,128
Coleman:

136
00:04:15,128 --> 00:04:19,830
Yes – we do at least one song with words and chords.

137
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:23,250
I mean that’s harder than you think.

138
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:23,589
Woman:

139
00:04:23,589 --> 00:04:25,620
Oh, I’m sure it is!

140
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:26,068
Coleman:

141
00:04:26,068 --> 00:04:30,099
That part of the lesson takes about 15 minutes.

142
00:04:30,099 --> 00:04:36,370
He often brings a recording of the song and plays it to us first.

143
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:36,777
Coleman:

144
00:04:36,777 --> 00:04:42,889
Then he hands out the song and if there’s a new chord in it,

145
00:04:42,889 --> 00:04:47,370
we practise that before we play it together – but really slowly.

146
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:47,853
Woman:

147
00:04:47,853 --> 00:04:50,270
Do you do any fingerpicking?

148
00:04:50,270 --> 00:04:50,630
Coleman:

149
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:53,873
That’s the last ten minutes of the lesson,

150
00:04:53,873 --> 00:04:58,918
when we pick out the individual notes from a tune he’s made up.

151
00:04:58,918 --> 00:05:00,720
It’s always quite simple.

152
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:01,058
Woman:

153
00:05:01,058 --> 00:05:02,750
That must be hard, though.

154
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:03,216
Coleman:

155
00:05:03,216 --> 00:05:04,148
It is,

156
00:05:04,148 --> 00:05:11,135
but people like it because they can really concentrate and if we’re all playing

157
00:05:11,135 --> 00:05:18,123
well, it sounds quite impressive.The only trouble is that he sometimes gets us to

158
00:05:18,123 --> 00:05:21,850
play one at a time – you know, alone.

159
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:22,368
Woman:

160
00:05:22,368 --> 00:05:23,920
That’s scary.

161
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:24,325
Coleman:

162
00:05:24,325 --> 00:05:25,134
It is,

163
00:05:25,134 --> 00:05:28,371
but I’ve got used to it now.

164
00:05:28,371 --> 00:05:34,036
At the end he spends about five minutes telling us what to practise for

165
00:05:34,036 --> 00:05:35,250
the following week.

166
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:36,136
Woman:

167
00:05:36,136 --> 00:05:38,792
Well, thanks Coleman.

168
00:05:38,792 --> 00:05:49,420
I’ll go and have a look at that website, I think.
